Package: erhai-device-support
Version: @VERSION@
Architecture: all
Maintainer: @MAINTAINER@
Installed-Size: @INSTALLED_SIZE@
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Depends: gnome-settings-daemon (= 50.0-1ubuntu1+piano2), gnome-settings-daemon-common (= 50.0-1ubuntu1+piano2), systemd, python3, python3-gi, gir1.2-glib-2.0, bluez, libglib2.0-bin, tuned-ppd (>= 2.25.1), systemd-zram-generator, erhai-kernel-zram, erhai-display-power (>= 0.1.0~)
Pre-Depends: dpkg (>= 1.17.14)
Description: OnePlus Pad 2 Pro (erhai) device support
 Everything the mainline port needs in the running system that is neither a
 firmware blob nor a kernel: the dual-boot switcher that rotates the Android
 and Ubuntu boot triplets through the active A/B slot, the store it keeps
 under /var/lib/erhai/switch, a desktop entry and polkit action for the
 Android direction, the display and power settings this tablet has been
 measured to keep, and the Bluetooth and Wi-Fi addresses this unit answers to.
 .
 The combo chip's NVM carries one address for every tablet of this model, so
 the kernel marks the controller unconfigured and BlueZ ignores it.
 erhai-bt-address derives an address for this unit from hardware it already
 carries, stores it in /var/lib/erhai/bt-address and hands it to the
 controller through the kernel's management interface before bluetoothd
 starts. The address is machine-specific data: it is written on the tablet at
 first boot and is in no package. It speaks to the kernel directly rather than
 running btmgmt, which waits forever for input it will never get when its
 stdin is not a terminal, as it never is under systemd.
 .
 The Wi-Fi radio has the same problem and a worse symptom: with no per-unit
 calibration its firmware answers out of the Atheros default OUI and invents
 the rest of the address afresh at every boot, so the tablet is a different
 machine to every DHCP reservation and MAC filter each time it comes up.
 erhai-wifi-address derives a stable address for this unit by its own scheme,
 stores it in /var/lib/erhai/wifi-address, and a udev rule applies it to the
 radio's interface. It has to be udev rather than a service: NetworkManager
 ignores a netdev until udev has finished with it, so there is no window in
 which the interface is managed with the firmware's address. A drop-in keeps
 NetworkManager from putting the firmware's address back.
 .
 It also disables flash-kernel (/etc/flash-kernel/machine = "none", the tool's
 own documented switch). The distribution's initramfs hooks call it whenever a
 kernel or firmware trigger fires, it has no database entry for this tablet,
 and it would have nothing to do if it had one: the boot images are built by
 erhai-kernel and written by erhai-switch. Left enabled it fails and takes
 dracut's configure down with it.
 .
 The Gunyah hardware watchdog stays disabled by default. It did not reset
 this tablet during the controlled test, so it cannot be relied on for
 recovery from a hard lockup. The optional configuration in
 /usr/share/erhai-device-support/watchdog is for driver development only.
 .
 TuneD provides the three GNOME power modes with board CPU/GPU limits.
 Compressed swap uses systemd-zram-generator and needs the zstd-enabled zram
 module from erhai 0.7.0 or later. Reboot after upgrading the kernel and this
 package together.
 .
 No exact kernel build is required. The boot images belong to
 erhai-kernel, which drives this package's installer from its own postinst.
